@@ replace-pre FIGURE-06
<figure class="fig">
<svg viewBox="0 0 900 318" role="img" aria-label="Three timelines of step A followed by step B, whose scratch shares bytes. With the step-end sync, B starts after A's kernels end. Without it, A's node finishes after its host part, and B's kernels run on the shared bytes while A's are still running. With event chaining, B's kernels wait on A's event and are safe, but B's host write to pinned scratch overlaps A's pending copy from it.">
  <defs>
    <marker id="cmh" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path class="hot" d="M0,1 L10,5 L0,9 z"/></marker>
  </defs>
  <!-- panel 1: with the sync -->
  <text class="b" x="20" y="16">With the step-end sync: safe</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="38">host</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="62">A's stream</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="86">B's stream</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="160" y="26" width="90" height="18"/><text class="s" x="205" y="39" text-anchor="middle">run A</text>
  <rect class="f-wait" x="250" y="26" width="180" height="18"/><text class="m s" x="340" y="39" text-anchor="middle">wrapper syncs A's stream</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="190" y="50" width="240" height="18"/><text class="s" x="310" y="63" text-anchor="middle">A's kernels, on the shared bytes</text>
  <line class="f-cap" x1="430" y1="22" x2="430" y2="94"/>
  <text class="s" x="436" y="20">A's node finishes</text>
  <rect class="f-la" x="440" y="26" width="90" height="18"/><text class="s" x="485" y="39" text-anchor="middle">run B</text>
  <rect class="f-la" x="470" y="74" width="230" height="18"/><text class="s" x="585" y="87" text-anchor="middle">B's kernels, the same bytes</text>

  <!-- panel 2: without -->
  <text class="b" x="20" y="122">Without it, the negative control: 2395 of 3000 runs collide</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="144">host</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="168">A's stream</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="192">B's stream</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="160" y="132" width="90" height="18"/><text class="s" x="205" y="145" text-anchor="middle">run A</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="190" y="156" width="240" height="18"/><text class="s" x="250" y="169" text-anchor="middle">A's kernels</text>
  <line class="f-cap" x1="250" y1="128" x2="250" y2="200"/>
  <text class="s" x="358" y="145">A's node finished at the line; its kernels run on</text>
  <rect class="f-la" x="260" y="132" width="90" height="18"/><text class="s" x="305" y="145" text-anchor="middle">run B</text>
  <rect class="f-la" x="290" y="180" width="230" height="18"/><text class="s" x="470" y="193" text-anchor="middle">B's kernels</text>
  <rect class="f-hot" x="290" y="154" width="140" height="46" stroke-width="2.2"/>
  <text class="s" x="530" y="168">both in use on the same bytes</text>

  <!-- panel 3: event chaining, pinned -->
  <text class="b" x="20" y="228">Event chaining instead: device scratch safe, pinned not</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="250">host</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="274">A's stream</text>
  <text class="m s" x="20" y="298">B's stream</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="160" y="238" width="90" height="18"/><text class="s" x="205" y="251" text-anchor="middle">A writes pinned</text>
  <rect class="f-int" x="190" y="262" width="250" height="18"/><text class="s" x="300" y="275" text-anchor="middle">kernels, then the copy from pinned</text>
  <line class="f-cap" x1="250" y1="234" x2="250" y2="306"/>
  <rect class="f-la" x="260" y="238" width="110" height="18"/><text class="s" x="315" y="251" text-anchor="middle">B writes pinned</text>
  <rect class="f-wait" x="290" y="286" width="150" height="18"/><text class="m s" x="365" y="299" text-anchor="middle">waits on A's event</text>
  <rect class="f-la" x="440" y="286" width="200" height="18"/><text class="s" x="540" y="299" text-anchor="middle">B's kernels: after A's, safe</text>
  <rect class="f-hot" x="258" y="236" width="182" height="46" stroke-width="2.2"/>
  <path class="f-hot" marker-end="url(#cmh)" d="M560,252 L446,252"/>
  <text class="s" x="566" y="256">the host write is on no stream: it overtakes A's copy</text>
</svg>
<figcaption>A and B share scratch bytes, which the closure allows since a path orders
them. What makes that safe is the wrapper's sync: a finished node is finished device
work. The model's negative control drops it and finds two byte-sharing regions in use at
once in 2395 of 3000 runs, so the simulation can see a collision, and sees none in 3500
runs with the sync. The third row is not in the model: the reason a cheaper replacement for
the sync is a change to the scratch rule too (<a href="#step-scratch">above</a>).</figcaption>
</figure>
